\f1\b\fs41 \cf0 Chapter 1. Getting Started\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb207

\f0\b0\fs20 \cf0 This chapter describes the purpose and use of this document, lists other related documents, and presents the conventions used within.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl449\sb259

\f1\b\fs34 \cf0 Purpose and Intended Audience\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb173

\f0\b0\fs20 \cf0 This section explains the intent of this document and describes its usage.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl374\sb216

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Purpose of the Exercises\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb144

\f0\b0\fs20 \cf0 The purpose of this guide is to teach the user of EDGE and BridgePoint Generator to understand how archetype files interact with the Generator and are used to produce target code from a xtUML model. Essential tasks that this guide will enable the user to perform include the following. \
\pard\tx200\pardeftab720\li200\fi-200\ri0\sl260\sb100

\fs16 \cf0 \'95	
\fs20 Create an archetype.\

\fs16 \'95	
\fs20 Process an archetype using EDGE.\

\fs16 \'95	
\fs20 Understand the model compiler meta-model.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb100
\cf0  \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl374\sb216

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Intended Audience\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb144

\f0\b0\fs20 \cf0 This guide is written for software engineers who are using the EDGE environment to create and use xtUML models and the MC-3020 model compiler. It assumes that the user is familiar with the ANSI C programming language and general usage of the development platform and its software development utilities. \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl374\sb216

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Additional Resources\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb144

\f0\b0\fs20 \cf0 The following additional documentation will be helpful to the reader: \
\pard\tx200\pardeftab720\li200\fi-200\ri0\sl260\sb100

\fs16 \cf0 \'95	
\fs20 Object Action Language Manual (
\f2\fs18 bpalref.pdf 
\f0\fs20 )\

\fs16 \'95	
\fs20 Nucleus BridgePoint Modeling Suite - Design Guide (
\f2\fs18 design.pdf
\f0\fs20 )\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb100
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl374\sb216

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Archetypes
\f0\b0\fs20 \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb100
\cf0 An archetype is a fragment of data access and text manipulation logic that states formally how to embed an Executable UML model into text. In more specific terms, an archetype is a rule that the BridgePoint model compiler will follow to produce C, or other types of code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl374\sb216

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Running Your Archetype\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb144

\f0\b0\fs20 \cf0 EDGE performs all of the code generation underneath the 
\f2\fs18 gen
\f0\fs20  folder in your project. In these exercises, you will create a number of archetype files. We suggest creating a new archetype for each exercise named 
\f2\fs18 ex1.arc
\f0\fs20 , 
\f2\fs18 ex2.arc
\f0\fs20 , etc... These should be saved into the folder 
\f2\fs18 my arcs
\f0\fs20  (or whatever you want to call it when you create it) in your project. Then copy the desired archetype to your 
\f2\fs18 gen
\f0\fs20  folder when you want to run it. \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb100
\cf0 When you select 
\f1 Build Project
\f0  for your project, EDGE will look for an archetype (
\f2\fs18 *.arc
\f0\fs20 ) file in the 
\f2\fs18 gen
\f0\fs20  folder, if it finds one, it will load the model into the "gen database", run the archetype, and then exit without finishing the rest of the model compilation process.\
Before proceeding, you will need to create a new xtUML project in EDGE and load a model with some data into the project. Request a sample model from your instructor if you do not already have one.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl539\sb311

