\fs20 Understand where data is kept in the gen database. \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb100
\cf0  \
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl374\sb216

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Task\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb144

\f0\b0\fs20 \cf0 Create a report on the screen (using the 
\i .print
\i0  operation) that shows the names of all the classes in any model.\
<RTR> You will first need to develop a set of objects (keyword 
\i O_OBJ
\i0  in the metamodel) that you can then obtain the instance name from. A 
\i .select many
\i0  operation will be required, as this will be a set of objects to iterate over and obtain the names. \
After instructing the Generator to assemble a set of objects, an operation to iterate across the set and print out the names is needed. A 
\i .for each
\i0  operation works in much the same way that a \'93for each\'94 loop works in other languages: each item in the set (
\i obj_set
\i0 ) will be set as the current object (
\i obj_inst
\i0 ) when the loop reaches its position in the set. Keep in mind that the 
\i obj_inst.Name
\i0  operation should be used as a substitution variable, enclosed in curly braces in order to be interpolated correctly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl374\sb216

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Things to consider...\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri0\sl260\sb144

